# Project Rules

## Autonomy
- Work autonomously and make reasonable technical decisions without asking me to choose tools, skills, agents or workflows.
- Use available specialized skills, agents and workflows when they materially improve the task, including ECC when installed.
- Delegate specialized or independent work when useful; use parallel delegation when appropriate.
- Do not delegate trivial work when delegation adds overhead.
- Integrate and verify delegated results yourself.

## Context & Tokens
- Minimize unnecessary context and token usage.
- Read only relevant files, code, logs and documentation.
- Prefer targeted inspection over full-project scans.
- Do not reread unchanged material or repeat successful checks without a reason.
- Stop investigating when sufficient evidence exists to proceed safely.
- Never perform a check merely because it is available.

## Verification
- Match verification depth to the scope and risk of the change.
- Start with modified code and direct dependencies; expand only when justified.
- Run the smallest meaningful test or validation.
- Do not run the full test suite, broad audits or extensive reviews for every change.
- Increase validation for complex, security-sensitive or high-impact changes.
- Never claim a check was performed if it was not.

## Visual Checks
- For UI changes, verify only the affected visual area.
- Prefer targeted screenshots/browser inspection over full-page capture.
- Do not recheck unchanged UI.
- For non-visual changes, skip visual verification.

## Framework & Security
- Respect project-specific instructions and installed framework versions.
- For Next.js, follow `AGENTS.md` and relevant local Next.js documentation.
- Treat authentication, authorization, databases, APIs, Supabase, secrets and external integrations as security-sensitive.
- Use targeted security validation when justified.

## Implementation
- Make the smallest correct change.
- Preserve existing architecture and conventions.
- Avoid unrelated refactoring and unnecessary dependencies.
- When debugging, identify the root cause before changing code.
- For complex tasks, plan first; for simple tasks, execute directly.

## Completion
- Before finishing, verify the result at a level appropriate to the task.
- Stop when the requested behavior works and further checks would provide little additional confidence.
- Do not continue scanning, testing or refactoring merely for completeness.

**Priority: autonomy, proportional verification, minimal context usage, and correctness.**